GPIO CONTROL - SAFE AGENT DECISION PROCEDURE

Inspect wiring and device ownership before changing output.
This board uses GP0..GP15 for 8x8 LED matrix, GP16/17 switches,
GP18 buzzer, GP19 servo motor, GP20/21 UART1, and GP26/27 I2C1.
GP25 drives the LED on standard Pico only, not on Pico W. These
attachments describe the teaching setup, not all Pico boards.

DISCOVER
  pins
  ls /dev/gpio
  ls /dev
  help pins
  help gpio

GPIO DEVICE NODES
  /dev/gpio/N can be a live digital input or output.
  A read may produce a continuous 0/1 stream, so use
  Use a bounded read tool if available. A hexdump offset may
  require lseek which streaming GPIO nodes might not support.
  Do NOT use an unbounded cat.
  Do NOT write to a pin without knowing direction, board wiring,
  and whether that pin is already claimed by an active driver.

INTERACTIVE TOOL
  gpio         full-screen UI, not safe through MCP execute_command
  pins         finite read-only text status: preferred

PROCEDURE
  1) identify the exact attached module and pin;
  2) call pins and list /dev devices;
  3) read relevant /man/devices or help for exact command syntax;
  4) get user's approval before setting outputs;
  5) apply one bounded operation;
  6) verify via pins or a bounded read.

A GP NUMBER IS NOT AN I/O VOLTAGE RATING.
Never use software guesses to drive an unknown physical circuit.


FBCHAR fixed-display device (/dev/fbchar)
----------------------------------------
Write exactly one printable ASCII character (32..126), or one of the
case-sensitive pattern names: heart, star, smile, sad, up, down, left,
right, check, cross, clear. The command `echo A > /dev/fbchar` displays
A; `echo heart > /dev/fbchar` draws a heart; `echo clear > /dev/fbchar`
clears the 8x8 matrix. The display persists after close(). The device rejects unknown names and does not scroll text.
Read with `cat /dev/fbchar` to list available patterns and usage;
the listing is finite and does not change the display.
Unlike /dev/fb (8 binary bytes), /dev/fbchar accepts ASCII.
Do not run scroll or other framebuffer writers concurrently. Python
may open the device for writing and call write(fd, "heart").
